## Migration: `TimedExecutor` → .NET 8

Your code uses the **timeout enforcement** pattern — `Thread.Abort()` kills a thread that exceeds a time limit. In .NET 6+, `Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`.

### Pattern Classification

| API | Issue |
|-----|-------|
| `thread.Abort()` | Throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` (`SYSLIB0006`) |
| `catch (ThreadAbortException)` | Never thrown in modern .NET |
| `Thread.ResetAbort()` | Also throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` |

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class TimedExecutor
{
    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
    {
        return ExecuteWithTimeout(work, timeout, CancellationToken.None);
    }

    public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<T> work, TimeSpan timeout, CancellationToken externalToken)
    {
        using var cts = CancellationTokenSource.CreateLinkedTokenSource(externalToken);
        cts.CancelAfter(timeout);

        var task = Task.Run(() => work(), cts.Token);

        try
        {
            // Task.WaitAsync throws OperationCanceledException if token fires
            return task.WaitAsync(cts.Token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
        }
        catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
        }
        catch (AggregateException ex) when (ex.InnerExceptions.Count == 1)
        {
            throw new AggregateException(ex.InnerException!);
        }
    }
}
```

### Critical Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---------|--------|
| **Work doesn't actually stop** | Unlike `Thread.Abort`, cancellation is *cooperative*. If `work()` is a tight CPU loop or blocking I/O that never checks the token, it keeps running even after timeout. You must pass `CancellationToken` into the work delegate. |
| **Leaked tasks** | After timeout, the `Task.Run` delegate may still be executing in the background. It won't be forcibly killed. Design work to check `token.IsCancellationRequested` at regular intervals. |
| **Not disposing `CancellationTokenSource`** | Leaks timer handles. Always wrap in `using`. |
| **Uncooperative code** | If `work()` calls code you can't modify (e.g., third-party library with no token support), the only true "kill" option is to run it in a **separate process** and call `Process.Kill()`. |

### Making Work Cooperate

For the cancellation to actually stop work, pass the token through:

```csharp
var executor = new TimedExecutor();

var result = executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(() =>
{
    // BAD: tight loop ignores cancellation — runs forever after timeout
    while (true) { /* compute */ }
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
```

Instead:

```csharp
// Add an overload that passes the token to work
public T ExecuteWithTimeout<T>(Func<CancellationToken, T> work, TimeSpan timeout)
{
    using var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(timeout);
    var token = cts.Token;
    var task = Task.Run(() => work(token), token);

    try
    {
        return task.WaitAsync(token).GetAwaiter().GetResult();
    }
    catch (OperationCanceledException) when (cts.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        throw new TimeoutException($"Operation exceeded {timeout.TotalSeconds}s timeout");
    }
}

// Caller cooperates:
executor.ExecuteWithTimeout(token =>
{
    while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        // do work in chunks
        token.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
    }
    return result;
}, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));
```

### Key Mindset Shift

| .NET Framework | .NET 8 |
|---|---|
| **Preemptive** — runtime injects exception anywhere | **Cooperative** — code must voluntarily check token |
| `Thread.Abort()` instantly tears down the thread | `cts.Cancel()` sets a flag; code must respond |
| `ThreadAbortException` auto-re-raises after catch | `OperationCanceledException` is a normal exception |

### References

- [SYSLIB0006 — Thread.Abort is obsolete](https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/fundamentals/syslib-diagnostics/syslib0006)
- [Cancellation in managed threads](https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/standard/threading/cancellation-in-managed-threads)
- [`Task.WaitAsync`](https://learn.microsoft.com/dotnet/api/system.threading.tasks.task.waitasync) (.NET 6+)
